          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which conditions here require a second code, and does the order the two are listed in matter?",
          "answer": "Here is the previous answer with the required disclaimer added. Nothing else has changed.\n\n**Conditions the passages mention:**\n- **Diabetes (E11 family, from \"Coding type 2 diabetes\"):** Diabetic chronic kidney disease is paired with a code for the stage of kidney disease. A diabetic foot ulcer is paired with a code for its site and depth.\n- **Heart failure (I50, from \"Coding heart failure\"):** Where heart disease is documented as hypertensive, the combination code for hypertensive heart disease is reported first, and a heart failure code is added to show the type of heart failure. The heart failure code is added, not swapped in.\n- **Hypertension (I10, from \"Coding hypertension\"):** I10 is a complete code with no further characters, so it needs no second code.\n\n**Does order matter?** Sometimes. The general conventions document (\"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\") says \"code first\" and \"use additional code\" fix the order: the underlying condition is reported first. \"Code also\" means two codes are needed but doesn't say which comes first. The retrieved text on the Excludes notes was cut off, so I can't tell you what it says about when two codes can't be reported together.\n\n**Limits:** The passages don't cover every condition that needs a second code, and they don't say which codes apply to a specific case. Code assignment depends on the full medical record. The sources also note that codes change over time, so check against the current official code files or a trained medical coder before relying on any of this.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
